## Testing standard

- A feature is done when tests prove it works the way a user uses it. Green service-level tests alone are not enough: they have hidden buttons that end in a 403, guards bypassed through another page and steps a user could skip.
- For every feature cover the happy path; every role that may act and every role that may not; validation and failure modes; edge cases (empty states, boundaries, duplicates); and side effects (persisted state, audit or log entries, notifications, queued jobs).
- Every user-facing flow also gets a browser test that drives the real UI: sign in, navigate, click, fill in, assert what the user sees.
- Name tests by behaviour in domain language, one behaviour per test. When a test exposes a bug or an unfinished feature, say so explicitly.
- Under `RefreshDatabase`, Laravel's testing transaction manager treats transaction level 1 as the outermost: after-commit work (`DB::afterCommit()`, jobs, listeners and notifications marked `afterCommit`) runs as soon as a nested transaction commits back to level 1, even while an application transaction on that level is still open. A test can't show that such work waits for an outer transaction. Code that wraps a long run in one transaction (a seeder, an import) must not expect queued after-commit jobs to have run before it ends: run that work inline, or outside the transaction.
- In a parallel run Laravel checks before each test that the process's database exists, and any database error in that check (a refused or lost connection, a restart of the database server) makes it drop the database and create it again, empty: `ensureTestDatabaseExists()` catches every `QueryException`. `RefreshDatabase` migrates once per process and doesn't migrate it again, so every later test of that process fails with a missing table. Reset its flag in the callback Laravel runs after such a re-creation, `ParallelTesting::setUpTestDatabaseBeforeMigrating()` (set `RefreshDatabaseState::$migrated` to false), registered in the base test case's `createApplication()`: Laravel runs these callbacks before `setUp()`. Then only the test caught by the failure fails. Never restart the database server during a run; a setting that takes effect on a reload needs none.
- `--processes=N` fixes the number of parallel processes; `--max-processes=N` caps ParaTest's default of one process per logical core. Put the cap, not a fixed count, in commands and scripts that run on machines with different numbers of cores.
- PHPUnit runs `tearDown()` also when `setUp()` threw, and Pest runs its `afterEach()` closures there, before the base test case's own. A boot refused in `createApplication()` after `Kernel::bootstrap()` leaves `$this->app` null, so Laravel's teardown never flushes that application, while `app()`, the facades, `tenancy()` and Eloquent's connection resolver still reach the refused application: a cleanup that deletes tenants, drops databases or removes files does it on the databases and disks that application names. A configuration cache (`php artisan optimize` or `config:cache` in the checkout) names the development ones: a test boot loads `bootstrap/cache/config.php` like any other, ignoring phpunit.xml's `<env>` values and `.env.testing`, and without a check `RefreshDatabase` runs `migrate:fresh` on the development database (a variable exported in the shell beats phpunit.xml too, even with `force="true"`). Refuse anything but the test environment and database names in `createApplication()`, right after the bootstrap (after `parent::setUp()` is too late), reading each name as Laravel connects (a connection's `url`, such as `DB_URL` from `.env`, overrides its `database` key), and record each application the check accepted in a `WeakMap` keyed by the object. Let every destructive cleanup throw an ordinary exception unless `app()` is one of them (an assertion failure in teardown would replace the boot's error in the report): `tearDown()`, `afterEach()` and the helpers they call, which can't read the protected `$this->app`. Don't check the names again there: a parallel worker's database becomes `<name>_test_<token>` after the boot. Call `parent::tearDown()` in a `finally`, and set `APP_CONFIG_CACHE`, `APP_ROUTES_CACHE` and `APP_EVENTS_CACHE` in phpunit.xml to files of their own, so the caches `optimize` writes never reach the tests (package caches, such as Filament's component cache, still need `php artisan optimize:clear`; reference code: the `stancl-tenancy-development` skill's isolation tests).
- Laravel resets `scoped()` bindings (and `#[Scoped]` classes) only in `forgetScopedInstances()`, which the queue worker's daemon runs before each job (and Octane between requests). Nothing in a test does for `get()` and `post()`, each `Livewire::test()` render, `set()` and `call()`: they run through the HTTP kernel of the test's one application, and a tenant switch resets nothing either. pest-plugin-browser's in-process server did the same up to 5.1; since 5.2 it forgets after each request the scoped instances that request resolved, but keeps those resolved before it (at boot, in `setUp()`, by the test body). A value cached in a scoped binding (a badge count, a setting read once per request, `Cache::memo()`, bound per store as `cache.__memoized:<store>` around the store it first resolved) then answers every later request of the test, and other tenants, with what was read first. PHP-FPM builds the application anew for each request and never shows it, but a command or request that loops over tenants (`runForMultiple()`) does: key your own such cache by tenant, and let the cache bootstrapper forget the `Cache::memo()` bindings on every switch (keys that name the tenant don't help there: the memo keeps reading and writing through the first tenant's store). In the base test case, forget the binding after every request in a `$this->app->terminating()` callback, which runs after all three kinds (also when a request starts, where the test body may resolve it first; a test that binds a double for it binds it again): `forgetInstance()`, plus `Facade::clearResolvedInstance()` for a facade in front of it, which keeps its own copy. Never call `forgetScopedInstances()` there: facades keep serving the old instances while `app()` hands out new ones (`SetUpPanel` sets the current panel through the `Filament` facade, Filament's views read `filament()`); with the facades cleared as well, Filament replays every registration ever made into the new managers, an earlier panel's render hooks included; and it discards what tests bound for scoped services (`instance()` and `mock()` doubles) and Filament's `NotificationMailbox`, which `assertNotified()` reads. A memo on `request()->attributes` is fresh in every test request but fits only code that runs in HTTP requests alone: in a queue worker or a command `request()` is one object for the whole process.
@if($assist->hasPackage('pestphp/pest'))
- Pest invokes closures in a dataset before passing them, except to a parameter typed `Closure`, which receives the closure itself. Don't wrap it in a second closure.
- Pest loads every test file into one process. Two files declaring a global helper function of the same name each pass alone and break the whole suite with a fatal redeclaration error. Give file-local helpers distinctive names and keep shared ones in `tests/Pest.php`; `grep -rhoE "^function \w+" tests | sort | uniq -d` lists clashes.
- `expect($object)->value` is the expectation's own `value` property (the object itself), not the object's `value`. Write `expect($object->value)`.
- `php artisan test --parallel --shard=k/n` fails: artisan passes its `--runner` option on to the `--list-tests` call Pest's sharding makes, and PHPUnit rejects it. Shard with `vendor/bin/pest --parallel --processes=N --shard=k/n`, which uses Laravel's parallel runner just the same. Pest splits shards by test file, not by run time: when one shard runs far longer than the others, record timings with `--update-shards`.
- Pest runs no test from a `<file>` entry of a phpunit.xml test suite: `--testsuite` lists and runs none of them, without a warning (seen with Pest 5.2 and PHPUnit 13). Build test suites, such as named groups for CI jobs, from `<directory>` entries, and guard the grouping with a test that holds every test file to exactly one suite and forbids single files, or tests drop out of CI unnoticed.
@endif
@if($assist->hasPackage('livewire/livewire', '^4.0'))

@scoped(['tests/**'])
## Livewire tests

- A component under `Livewire::test()` runs `assertSee()`, `assertDontSee()`, `assertSeeText()`, `assertDontSeeText()`, `assertSeeHtml()`, `assertDontSeeHtml()` and `assertSeeHtmlInOrder()` against its current HTML. Every other response assertion, `assertSeeInOrder()` and `assertSeeTextInOrder()` among them, goes to the last HTTP response: it passes on the first render and fails after `set()` or `call()`, whose response is the update's JSON (escaped markup such as `<\/p>` and `\u00fc`, plus the snapshot). Check order with `assertSeeHtmlInOrder()` (texts through `e()`) or in `$component->html()`.
- `Livewire::withQueryParams()`, `withCookies()` and `withHeaders()` stay set for every later `Livewire::test()` in the same test. Call `withQueryParams()` before each `test()`, with an empty array where none apply, or a later component starts with the earlier ones.
- A page whose record doesn't exist, or that a resource query hides, answers `Livewire::test()` with a 404 response, not an exception: Laravel's handler turns the `ModelNotFoundException` into one. Assert `assertNotFound()`; `expect(fn () => …)->toThrow()` never sees an exception.
@endscoped
@endif
@if($assist->hasPackage('filament/filament', '^5.0'))

@scoped(['tests/**'])
## Filament tests

- Filament silently ignores a request to mount or call an action the user can't see (hidden, unauthorized or not found): no 403, no exception. A test that calls a forbidden action and checks that nothing changed also passes when the action's name is wrong, so also assert the action hidden for that user and visible for one who may use it. The test helpers can't resolve a hidden action attached to a schema component (a section header, an entry) at all; assert that its label is missing from the page.
- A field's test key is its absolute key: a parent layout component's `key()` becomes part of it unless set with `isInheritable: false`, and field assertions such as `assertFormFieldExists()` then miss the field.
- `goToNextWizardStep()` sets the wizard's next step before validating the current one and leaves it set when `afterValidation()` throws `Halt`: `assertWizardCurrentStep()` then reports the next step although the browser stays, since the browser moves only on the `next-wizard-step` event. After a halted step assert `assertNotDispatched('next-wizard-step')`, and `assertDispatched('next-wizard-step')` where the step does move, so the negative check can fail.
- An action whose submission fails validation stays mounted on the test component. A second `callAction()` for it on the same component then looks for the action inside the mounted one and fails with "Action [name] was not found for action [name]". Test the refused and the successful submission on separate `Livewire::test()` instances.
- Since Filament 5.10 a sent notification does not sit in the session under `filament.notifications` any more: `Notification::send()` pushes it into `NotificationMailbox`, pending during the request and published to the cache under a scope the session holds (by the `PublishNotifications` middleware at the end of the request, or by `flush()`), and the page that shows it pulls it out. A test that reads the session key finds nothing. To inspect a notification beyond its title (`assertNotified()` compares titles), flush the mailbox, `pull()` the notifications and push them back for whoever reads them next (`filament-browser-testing` skill, `sentNotification()`). Since 5.10.3 `PublishNotifications` publishes a Livewire response's notifications under a batch only the tab that sent the request pulls (the `X-Filament-Notifications` header names it), and `pull()` without that batch returns only notifications without one. The recipe works under `Livewire::test()`, which runs no middleware, and after a request that is no Livewire response; after a Livewire request through the kernel (`postJson(Livewire::getUpdateUri())`), pass the header's batch to `pull()`.
@endscoped
@endif
@if($assist->hasPackage('pestphp/pest-plugin-browser'))

@scoped(['tests/**'])
## Browser tests (pest-plugin-browser)

- The in-process server shares the test's application, so the session store, auth guards, tenancy and Filament's current panel carry over from one request to the next unless the base test case resets them per request. Scoped services did too up to pest-plugin-browser 5.1; since 5.2 the server forgets after each request the scoped instances that request resolved, and clears the facades' cache, so only those resolved before a request (at boot, in `setUp()`, by the test body) carry over. One of them can be Filament's navigation (`NavigationManager`: kept, every page shows the first request's entries, badges and links; up to Filament 5.9 also the scoped `originalRequest`, which made the menu mark the first page of the session as the current one — a plain binding since 5.10). Livewire's per-request state too (`app('livewire')->flushState()` at the start of each request, as Livewire's test harness does between renders): its persistent-middleware bookkeeping skips a page's middleware for a snapshot of the route it last ran them for until that state is flushed (Livewire 4.4.8; before, for any route it had run them for), so a later Livewire request for the same page skips `SetUpPanel` and `Authenticate` and runs as whatever the previous request left — which shows since Filament 5.10 authorizes a lazy widget on hydrate. And Filament's current panel (`Filament::setCurrentPanel(null)`): kept, a request to one panel after a request to another starts as the other's until the panel middleware runs. The session store as well: the session manager builds it on first use and keeps it for the process, so a store touched before tenancy (a listener calling `session()`) is built in central context, with the central lifetime, and serves every tenant from there; forget the manager's drivers and the resolved `session.store` singleton, which the guards hold, at the start of each request, so `StartSession` builds the store after tenancy as a fresh process does — with database sessions in the tenant's own table and with its lifetime, where a list of signed-in devices reads real rows. `actingAs()` then signs in every browser context: don't mix it with signing in through the login form. Base test case and helpers: `filament-browser-testing` skill.
- While Vite's hot file exists (`public/hot`, written by `npm run dev` and removed when it exits; a dev server that died leaves it behind), the Blade `vite` directive and Filament's `viteTheme()` point every page's assets at the dev server, and the in-process server serves every request from the test's `Vite` singleton: browser tests then run against the dev server's live sources, or without styles and scripts once the file is stale, never against the build CI tests, and a content security policy limited to `'self'` reports or blocks every page. In the browser base test case's `setUp()`, after `parent::setUp()`, call `$this->withVite()` (it undoes a `withoutVite()` from the base test case, whose stand-in ignores the hot file) and then point the container's instance at a path nothing creates: `$this->app->make(Vite::class)->useHotFile(storage_path('framework/testing/vite.hot'))`. Build the assets before browser runs (`npm run build`, in CI too). A `withoutVite()` in a Pest `beforeEach()` that covers the browser tests runs after `setUp()` and undoes both.
- A click doesn't wait for a Livewire redirect. Assert something the next page shows before the next step.
- Playwright acts as soon as an element exists, and `assertSee()` and `assertPathIs()` pass while the next page is still loading. A field filled before Livewire starts is emptied when it starts (it writes the form's empty state into its fields), and a button clicked then does nothing. Up to pest-plugin-browser 5.1 a navigation also ended on any page's `load` event, including the late one of the page being left, so the next step could run on a page that hadn't loaded (since 5.2 `visit()`, `navigate()` and `refresh()` wait for their own page's `load`; a click still waits for nothing). Keep every page hidden until its `load` event in the base test case; assertions, fills and clicks then wait for the loaded page (`filament-browser-testing` skill). Without it, suites pass on fast machines and fail at random on CI. Filament starts many components only once their script has arrived after `load` (`x-load`: toggle buttons, the password reveal, tabs, the wizard, builders, selects, pickers, editors): until then a click on their buttons does nothing, and a value typed into a field inside is dropped when the binding sets the field from the component's data. So keep both out of reach until Alpine has bound them, as the reference base test case does: every `wire:model` field read-only (selects and checkboxes disabled) until it carries `_x_model`, and every `x-load` element `aria-disabled` until it carries `_x_dataStack`, which Playwright counts for everything inside; it waits for an enabled, editable element. An optional single-choice toggle option is a button without `wire:model`, so a guard that holds fields alone lets its click get lost (a required one is a `wire:model` radio, held like a checkbox). Mark elements from a `MutationObserver` the moment they are added, not from a poll: Playwright fills a window's new field between two rounds. Hold each for a bounded time from when it was added, not only during a page's first seconds, or windows opened later go unguarded. Not by hiding the page longer: Filament's upload field defers its own set-up while hidden and never resumes.
- Up to pest-plugin-browser 5.1 `navigate()` ran like an assertion, in attempts of one second, asking for the page again after each: a page slower than that (a heavy editor, a whole year's calendar, any page on a slow CI runner) failed with "Timeout 1000ms exceeded" or "Navigation to … is interrupted by another navigation", and every page after it in a sweep went unchecked; open such a page with one `$page->page()->goto($url)`, whose wait ended on any frame's first `load` event, and follow it with an assertion that polls the page itself (its path, `document.readyState`, Livewire started). Since 5.2 `navigate()`, `refresh()`, `back()` and `forward()` run once with the browser timeout and return when Playwright reports their own page loaded. Nothing retries them any more: a navigation interrupted by another one (a Livewire redirect still on its way from the previous click) fails at once, so assert what the previous step shows before navigating away. Playwright's timeout message ("Timeout 15000ms exceeded.") names no page: a check that visits page after page puts the URL into its failure (`filament-browser-testing` skill, `navigateOnce()`).
- pest-plugin-browser's `waitForEvent()` waits for nothing (5.1, still in 5.2): it calls `Page::waitForLoadState()`, which creates the client's request generator without iterating it, so the request never reaches Playwright. Wait for a page with an assertion that polls it.
- pest-plugin-browser clears its output folders when a run without `--parallel` loads its first browser test: it deletes what `glob('*')` finds in `tests/Browser/Screenshots` and its `Sliders` and `ImageDiffView` subfolders, then removes each folder with a silenced `rmdir()`. PHPUnit (13) counts a warning raised while the test files load even when it is silenced, so a dotfile there (a `.gitkeep`, which `glob('*')` skips) makes every run report a warning that names no test: Pest's own output shows nothing, laravel/pao's agent output lists it without details, and under `failOnWarning` a run whose tests all passed fails. Keep `tests/Browser/Screenshots` and `tests/Browser/Traces` out of version control through the root `.gitignore`, never through a file inside them. Up to 5.1 the plugin also removed `Traces`, `Sliders` and `ImageDiffView` without checking that they exist (one to three such warnings in nearly every run; create the three folders at the top of `tests/Pest.php`, which Pest loads before the test files); 5.2 skips missing folders and removes `Traces` only when it is empty.
- `script()` does not throw when a navigation replaces the page while it runs: it returns the browser's error text, a string, in place of the value. Since pest-plugin-browser 5.2 `visit()`, `navigate()` and `$page->page()->goto()` return once their own page has loaded, so a `script()` right after them reads that page. After a step that changes the page without waiting (a click, a Livewire redirect), read only once an assertion that polls the page has seen it loaded (its path, `document.readyState`, Livewire started); where the path stays the same, mark the page being left first (a property on `window`) and read until a wholly loaded page without the mark answers. Up to 5.1 a page opened with `navigate()` needed that mark too (`filament-browser-testing` skill, `readAfterNavigation()`).
- Since pest-plugin-browser 5.2 `assertNoJavaScriptErrors()` also counts unhandled promise rejections, reported by their message alone, without Chromium's "Uncaught " prefix ("[object Object]" for a rejected Livewire action chained with `then()` but no `catch()`). Headless Chromium refuses `navigator.clipboard.writeText()` without the clipboard permission: a copy button catches its promise (and shows "copied" only in `then()`).
- `script()` hands each JavaScript number back as the plugin's `json_decode()` reads Playwright's JSON (5.1, still in 5.2): an `int` when it is whole, a `float` when it is not, and negative zero (`Math.round(-0.3)`) as the string `'-0'`, inside a returned array or object too. Whether a measurement is whole depends on the layout, so `expect($page->script('….getBoundingClientRect().left'))->toBe(0.0)` fails whenever the element sits on a whole pixel, and `assertScript($expression, 64.0)` compares with `toBe()` as well: it retries until the browser timeout and then reports that 64 is not 64. Compare in the page and assert the boolean (`assertScript("Math.abs(document.querySelector('.box').getBoundingClientRect().left - 64) < 0.5")`; JavaScript has one number type, and `-0 === 0` there), or in PHP with `toEqualWithDelta()` once `toBeNumeric()` has passed: a `(float)` cast turns `null` (an `undefined` result, such as a missing element read with `?.`), `false` and any text without a leading number into `0.0`, and `toEqual()` and `toEqualWithDelta()` take `null` and `false` for `0`.
- `assertSee()` matches any visible text containing the string, case ignored (Playwright's `getByText`): `assertSee('Confirmed')` already passes on a prompt reading "… must be confirmed.". To wait for a state, assert the exact element: `assertVisible('text="Confirmed"')`.
- `assertSee()` does not find the text of the option a native `<select>` shows as chosen. Check the choice with `assertValue()` on the select.
- Never travel back in time in a browser test: the browser drops session cookies that expired in the travelled past.
- Reach tenants as `http://<subdomain>.localhost:<port>` (Chromium resolves every `*.localhost` to loopback) and set the tenant base domain to `localhost` before service providers boot.
- Select Filament fields by id: `[id="form.email"]`, `[id="mountedActionSchema0.name"]`. A dotted name is otherwise parsed as CSS. Searchable selects are a button plus an option list, not a native select.
- A Builder or Repeater is a field itself, wrapping its items' fields: a selector by label such as `.fi-fo-field:has(label:has-text("…"))` also matches the builder around the field, and so every field nested in it. Select nested fields by id (`[id*=".conditions."][id$=".field"]`). `nth=` waits only for a position that doesn't exist yet: a block inserted before others, or into a nested builder, is found at once at the old block's position, so select it by an id pattern too (`filament-browser-testing` skill).
- `click()`, `fill()` and the other element methods take a string that starts with `#`, `.` or `[`, or contains one of `: ( ) , = > + ~ * | ^`, or a dot before a letter ("z.B."), as a CSS selector, and anything else as visible text. Click such a text through Playwright's text engine, quoted for an exact match: `text="Temperature: 9.5 °C"`. Unquoted, it also matches every longer text containing it, and strict mode fails on two matches. `assertSee()` always searches text. A bare tag name is text too: `keys('body', …)` waits for an element showing "body"; press page-wide keys on an element found by class or id (`.fi-body` in a Filament panel).
- Playwright dismisses the browser's own dialogs, so a button with a confirm dialog (Livewire's `wire:confirm`) silently does nothing. Set `window.confirm` to answer true before the click (`filament-browser-testing` skill).
- pest-plugin-browser's in-process server handed the app a multipart request's raw body but none of its files up to 5.0; since 5.1 it parses the parts itself, but a nested name such as Livewire's `files[]` stays a flat key where PHP nests it, so every Livewire or Filament upload still arrives empty and fails (Livewire reports `Undefined array key 0`). Parse multipart bodies in the base test case into `Illuminate\Http\UploadedFile` objects created with `test: true`, in place of what the plugin found (a Symfony `UploadedFile` is rebuilt without its test flag, and validation then rejects a file PHP didn't upload), and create Livewire's test upload disk in `setUp()` with `FileUploadConfiguration::storage()`. Code and upload helpers: `filament-browser-testing` skill.
- Filament renders on the server for every round trip; raise the browser timeout above the 5-second default.
- A Filament button with `labeled-from` renders twice, icon-only and labelled, with one of them hidden at each screen width. A selector by its `href` or label matches both, and strict mode fails: add `:visible`.
- Run WebAuthn/passkey flows with a software authenticator so the server verifies real signatures; never stub the verification.
- Playwright refuses to click an element that another element covers ("… intercepts pointer events") and retries until the timeout, even when both are links to the same address. Where links are stretched over a card, stretch only one per card, and click that one.
- A Livewire lazy component (Filament's widgets, where Filament renders them) and a Filament upload field (FilePond, loaded when it becomes visible) appear only once scrolled into view. A check that measures or screenshots a whole page scrolls through it and waits for them first, or it measures their placeholders.
- Filament scrolls to the first validation error 200 ms after it appears; up to 5.10.1 the handler scrolls the error node's field wrapper without checking that the node is still on the page (5.10.2 checks). A browser test that corrects a refused form and resubmits within that window, so that the response without the error has morphed the node away, gets `Uncaught TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'scrollIntoView')`, once per schema handling the event, and `assertNoJavaScriptErrors()` fails or passes with the timing. Below 5.10.2, pause a moment after asserting the error before correcting the form, as a person reads it first.
- Since Filament 5.10 a single-choice ToggleButtons that is not `required()` renders each option as a `<button aria-pressed>` carrying the option's id (`<field id>-<value>`), with no radio input and no label: click the button, by id or by `button[aria-pressed]:has-text("…")`. A click on `label:has-text("…")` lands on something else or nothing, and the test times out later on what should have followed. Since 5.11 a `required()` single-choice field renders radio inputs instead (the option's id, `wire:model`, native `required`; invisible, without pointer events), each with a `<label>` styled as a button inside `role="radiogroup"`, and a multiple-choice field keeps checkbox inputs with labels: click the label, never the input. One selector fits both single-choice forms: `:is(button[aria-pressed], .fi-fo-toggle-buttons[role="radiogroup"] label):has-text("…")`. A required field left empty is then stopped by the browser's own validation bubble before any request, so a browser test never sees Filament's message for it, and a chosen option is no longer cleared by clicking it again.
- Since Filament 5.10 the sign-in's second step submits itself once the code's last digit is in (the challenge's `OneTimeCodeInput` carries `submitOnCompletion()`). A browser test that fills the code and then presses the confirm button waits for a button on a page that has already moved on: fill the code and assert what the next page shows.
@endscoped
@endif
